abstract: >-
  Matter grants every commissioned hub genuine authority over the device it
  controls: a hub can operate a lock, not merely relay a user's request.
  Certification verifies that a device conforms to the specification, never
  that a hub merits that authority.

  This is a drop-in Matter component that removes the hub from the device's
  trust boundary without giving up ecosystem interoperability. A
  manufacturer-specific cluster carries an opaque bidirectional byte tunnel;
  an application-layer signing layer authenticates payloads end-to-end in both
  directions, binding the device's tunnel key to its own Matter Device
  Attestation Certificate so a relaying hub can neither read nor forge them.

  The repository includes the component, a thin example application, an
  interleaved measurement harness, and the raw datasets behind the reported
  results: three transports at n=1000 each, showing that signing costs about
  40 ms end-to-end and that this cost is invariant to the transport carrying
  it, agreeing with an isolated on-device benchmark to within the width of a
  confidence interval. Decomposing the path shows a commercial hub contributes
  about 13% of round-trip latency against about 59% for Matter over Thread
  itself.
